WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.880
A customer calls support,

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.120
hears "press one for billing",
and presses a key.

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.360
Your line never sees a key, only samples.

4
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.480
The beep sounds like one note, and it's
two: a low tone for the key's row,

5
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.800
a high tone for its column.

6
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.600
Key five is seven seventy hertz plus

7
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.200
thirteen thirty-six. Which two?

8
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:15.560
Start smaller.

9
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.360
Eight samples
of a two thousand hertz tone:

10
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:18.900
one, zero, minus one, zero.

11
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.480
Multiply them by your own copy,
a test tone, and add up.

12
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.180
A two thousand hertz test scores four.

13
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.400
One thousand hertz scores exactly zero.

14
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.140
Now a nasty surprise.

15
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.840
The same tone,
a quarter turn behind, scores zero.

16
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.940
So take a backward wheel,
turning clockwise,

17
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.100
test with both its shadows,
left-right and up-down,

18
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.680
and treat the two sums as an arrow.

19
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.620
Its length is four, whatever the phase.

20
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.780
That length is the match.

21
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:40.280
Why?

22
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.780
Remember lesson six: a sample
is two half arrows laid tip to tail,

23
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.860
one turning forward,
its mirror turning backward.

24
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.720
Now lay each sample along a test wheel,

25
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.200
turning backward, clockwise,
at the test tone's speed.

26
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.120
The forward half stands still,

27
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.360
like a fan under a strobe,

28
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.680
and its arrows pile up:

29
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.740
N over two,
which is four in a block of eight.

30
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.540
The mirror half turns twice as fast, steps
back and forth, and ends where it started.

31
00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.350
Against the one thousand hertz wheel,

32
00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.730
the arrows close an octagon and end
where they began: exactly zero.

33
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.910
Any whole number of turns
of difference does the same.

34
00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.670
So test only with whole-turn wheels.

35
00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.050
Each one is a bin: bin k makes
k turns in a block of N samples.

36
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.870
The list of their matches is the DFT.

37
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.100
Eight samples put the bins
a thousand hertz apart,

38
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.120
so every row tone lands in bin one.

39
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.800
That spacing, eight thousand over N,

40
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.360
is the frequency resolution.

41
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.020
Close tones need a longer block.

42
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.980
At forty samples, seven seventy
and eight fifty-two share a bin.

43
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.180
At two hundred and five,
each tone gets its own bin,

44
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.820
and an empty one on each side.

45
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.620
A customer presses five.

46
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.040
Two hundred and five samples,

47
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.320
eight keypad bins.

48
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.640
Bin twenty scores ninety
percent of a full match,

49
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.840
bin thirty-four ninety-two,
the other six at thirteen percent or less.

50
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.120
Row seven seventy,
column thirteen thirty-six: the key five.

51
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.640
But silence has a strongest bin too.

52
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.120
So give the detector a bar
at twenty-five percent:

53
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.420
a key counts only when exactly
one row and one column clear it.

54
00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.580
Your turn, in the lab.

55
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.260
Start at forty samples and watch
keys five and eight collide.

56
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.280
Then lengthen the block
until they come apart,

57
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.080
and decode a customer's account number.

58
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.370
Then billing complains:
some voices fake a key.

59
00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.130
A real press has almost nothing
outside its two tones,

60
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.950
so the detector must check every bin.

61
00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.650
That costs one point six five million

62
00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:16.950
multiplications a second,

63
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.910
and the cheap chip
manages about a million.

64
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.050
That's the next bug.
